<Project>

  <!--
    Copied into the same scratch directory as the nuget.config beside it, and for the same
    reason: to make the throwaway consumer restore the package this repository just packed
    rather than a copy of some earlier build.

    That file sets globalPackagesFolder, which is the setting for this. This one exists
    because the setting is not the last word. NuGet reads the NUGET_PACKAGES environment
    variable ahead of it and does not warn when it takes it, so on a machine that exports one
    the isolation simply does not happen: restore succeeds, reports the machine folder, and
    the check grades whatever was extracted there first. NUGET_FALLBACK_PACKAGES is the same
    story one step along, and it survives the config clearing fallbackPackageFolders outright.
    Neither is exotic; pointing NUGET_PACKAGES at a cache directory is the ordinary way a CI
    job avoids re-downloading.

    RestorePackagesPath and RestoreFallbackFolders are MSBuild properties, which NuGet reads
    ahead of both the settings and the environment. So they are the layer where this can be
    stated rather than hoped for, and they are why the run does not have to ask what the shell
    it started in happens to export.

    Both values point at this file's own directory, so the folder lands beside it wherever the
    directory was copied to and there is nothing here to edit. Unconditional on purpose: a
    condition is how a line like this stops firing in the one case it exists for.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RestorePackagesPath>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)packages</RestorePackagesPath>
    <RestoreFallbackFolders>clear</RestoreFallbackFolders>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
